Discourse:
"A ruler takes the entire realm as his measure."

Imperial Decrees, Mandates, and Memorials (candidates are examined on one):
A simulated Han dynasty "Decree Recommending the Worthy and Upright and Men of Literature" (first year of the Yuanguang era [134 BCE]);
A simulated Tang dynasty "Mandate Appointing Lu Zhi 陸贄 as Vice Director of the Secretariat and Jointly Manager of Affairs with the Secretariat-Chancellery" (eighth year of the Zhenyuan era [792]);
A simulated Song dynasty "Memorial of Thanks from Fan Zuyu 范祖禹 upon Being Appointed Right Policy Critic and Concurrent Expositor-in-Waiting" (fourth year of the Yuanyou era [1089]).

Five Legal Judgments:
"Households Are Determined by Registration";
"Performing Sacrifices to Deities in the Sacrificial Canon";
"Delays in Official Business That Should Be Executed";
"Jointly Convened Litigation Involving Military and Civilian Personnel";
"Repairing Bridges and Roads."

Third Session

Five Policy Questions:
Question: The means by which the sage emperors and enlightened kings of antiquity ascended to flourishing virtue and brightened their great enterprises were in every case achieved through learning. Tang [Yao], Yu [Shun], and the Three Dynasties are remote in antiquity; what is praised in the Book of Odes (Shi) and the Book of Documents (Shu)…
